WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
LARRY ARNN: We all think if
we were rich, we'd be happy.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.080
But also we know we might not.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.860
And then a lot of people
who are rich are not happy.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.443 --> 00:00:15.610
LARRY ARNN: What did we say
was the ultimate purpose

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.910
of human life,
according to Aristotle?

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:19.600
ALL: Happiness.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.980
LARRY ARNN: Happiness.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.330
And we haven't quite
said what that is.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:27.440
So there's a bunch of claims
about what makes happiness.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.750
They're famous claims.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.420
JUAN: Wealth, honor.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:32.350
LARRY ARNN: Yeah.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:33.220
JUAN: Health.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:33.690
SPEAKER 1: Pleasure.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:34.240
JUAN: Pleasure.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.365
LARRY ARNN: Pleasure, yeah.

00:00:35.365 --> 00:00:37.440
So wealth is obvious, isn't it.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.940
I don't know if you know the
Saturday Night Live philosopher

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.100
Jack Handy, but
he's very profound.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.600
And one of his
aphorisms is, it's easy

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.450
wanting other people's money,
that's what I like about it.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:58.330
And we all think, don't we, if
we were rich, we'd be happy.

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.830
But also, by the way,
in our heart of hearts,

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:02.870
we know we might not.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.190
And then a lot of people
who are rich are not happy.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.310
And what else did you name?

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:08.990
JUAN: Pleasure.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.090
LARRY ARNN: Pleasure.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.660
Pleasure's good.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:12.280
We like it.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.050
Can you think, is it happiness?

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.180
What does Aristotle
say about that?

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:21.040
SPEAKER 2: He said, it's
the life of fatted cattle.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.620
LARRY ARNN: If you live
only for that and only

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.650
in the common sense
meaning of that,

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:30.400
it wouldn't be good for us if we
just pursued that exclusively.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.550
And we all know, something
Aristotle points out powerfully

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.860
in this book, we all
know that if you just

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.913
went after that
stuff, fully, that

00:01:38.913 --> 00:01:40.330
among the things
that would happen

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.290
is it wouldn't be very pleasant.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.800
See, I don't think
alcoholics take

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.930
a lot of pleasure in alcohol.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:53.240
I think, in fact, it's
a plague upon them.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.750
And I think that a lot
of things are like that.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.100
So pleasure won't do,
and wealth won't do.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.420
What else, Juan?

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:01.960
JUAN: Honor.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:03.910
LARRY ARNN: Honor,
now what about honor?

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:05.920
What's honor?

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.090
JUAN: Good regard from others.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.090
LARRY ARNN: Is that the
same thing as happiness,

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.427
being honorable?

00:02:12.427 --> 00:02:13.010
SPEAKER 1: No.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:13.880
LARRY ARNN: Why not?

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.650
SPEAKER 1: He says
that the people who--

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:18.440
or people who want to be
honored in the political life

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.410
want to be honored by
people who are virtuous.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.380
So what their-- their
aim is not just honor,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.140
but it's honor
from something they

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:29.600
value more than just
honor in itself.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.970
LARRY ARNN: Gil suggested
a complication in honor.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:38.430
There'll always
be the temptation

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:46.020
to value the honor as much
or more than the quality that

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.060
gives rise to it.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.820
What would you
say, Juan, is there

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.880
a difference in dignity
between those first two

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.000
things, pleasure and wealth,
and the claims of honor?

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.710
JUAN: They have
some good in them.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.700
Pleasure is not inherently bad.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:01.950
And the same thing for wealth.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.620
There is a place
for wealth, as well.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.343
And so I think the same
is for all three of them.

00:03:09.343 --> 00:03:11.010
LARRY ARNN: Yeah,
nobody's claiming here

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.870
that it wouldn't be
better to be rich.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.480
It just isn't enough.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:16.260
JUAN: Right.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.150
LARRY ARNN: And that's
true of everything else.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.340
Now, we go to page 10.

00:03:23.148 --> 00:03:24.690
OK, there's a
paragraph at the bottom

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.520
says, "but perhaps to say."

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.223
Somebody read that.

00:03:28.223 --> 00:03:30.390
SPEAKER 3: "But perhaps to
say that the highest good

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.840
is happiness is obviously
something undisputed, while it

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.540
still begs to be said in a more
clear and distinct way what

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.160
happiness is.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.660
Now this might
come about readily

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.940
if one were to grasp the
work of a human being."

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.120
LARRY ARNN: Grasp the
work of the human being.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.580
What does a human being do?

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:48.080
OK.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.372
SPEAKER 4: "But presumably
it makes no small difference

00:03:50.372 --> 00:03:52.500
whether one supposes the
highest good to consist

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.440
in possession or in use,
that is in an actively

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.470
maintained condition or in
a way of being at work."

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:02.040
LARRY ARNN: And what active
condition means is you

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.950
have gone through the process
of engraving on your soul

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:06.810
the possession of these virtues.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:14.790
And now, they are solid in you,
and you are manifesting them.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.709
They radiate from you.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:21.120
And what being at work means is
when the virtues are actually

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.019
engaged.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:24.570
And so the epitome of
the human experience

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:29.730
is this active condition
or habit of being at work

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:34.660
or expressing character when the
human being is fully engaged.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.518
One of the reasons
we like sport--

00:04:36.518 --> 00:04:38.310
there's a basketball
game tonight, I think.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.800
And basketball is
good, because you

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.590
can see them, both the
girls and the boys,

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.270
and you're close to them.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.790
And you see them under strain.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:54.180
And you see a long, practiced
capacity, an active condition

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.058
fully at work.

00:04:56.058 --> 00:04:57.600
And that's what we
like about sports.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.380
We like to see that.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:03.480
And that's because a human
being is excelling at something.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.360
What the human
being is made for is

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.610
those moments when its
qualities are fully engaged.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.240
Happiness is
ultimately an activity

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.470
of the soul that is
excellent in all of the ways

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:24.960
that the human being acts
and also all of the ways

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.900
that the human being thinks.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:32.910
And if you can be good
at all of those things,

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:37.350
you will certainly have a
wide measure of happiness.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.830
And to the extent that
you're not good in that way,

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.470
your happiness will
be compromised.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:46.890
And Aristotle presents that
as just a fact, inescapable.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.440
I'm Larry Arnn, president
of Hillsdale College.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.900
You will find if you look
at our online courses

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.540
that they cover the whole
range of human learning,

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.020
all the high and fundamental
things everyone needs to know.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.300
They are free,
because we have always

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.270
wished to provide sound
learning to anyone

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:09.390
who wishes to have it.

